The longest wavelength (threshold wavelength) \(\lambda_{\text{Th}}\) is related to the work function by the equation:
\frac{hc}{\lambda_{\text{Th}}} = \phi
Using the approximation \(hc = 12420\text{ eV}\cdot\mathring{A}\) (or \(1240\text{ eV}\cdot\text{nm}\)): 
\lambda_{\text{Th}} = \frac{hc}{\phi} = \frac{12420\text{ eV}\cdot\mathring{A}}{3\text{ eV}}
\lambda_{\text{Th}} = \frac{12420}{3} \mathring{A} = 4140 \mathring{A}
Converting Angstroms to nanometers (\(1\mathring{A} = 0.1\text{ nm}\)): 
\lambda_{\text{Th}} = 4140 \times 0.1 \text{ nm} = 414 \text{ nm}
Thus, the longest wavelength of light that can cause the emission of photoelectrons is approximately 414 nm.

